In semi-destructive disassembly, the designer accepts that certain connective elements (rivets, clips, adhesive bonds, press-fits) will be destroyed during disassembly, but designs the product so that only these low-value connective components are sacrificed while all functional components and materials are preserved intact. The key design principle is to concentrate all irreversible joints in sacrificial, low-cost connectors that can be easily and cheaply replaced during reassembly.